notes.body:
- Classic notebooks execute cells in whatever order you clicked them — the #1 source of 'works on my machine' bugs: hidden state where outputs don't match a top-to-bottom read.
- Marimo is reactive: cells form a dependency graph; changing a cell automatically reruns its dependents, always in a consistent order. The notebook IS the app (`marimo run`) and the script (`marimo export`).
- Every marimo notebook is stored as plain Python — git-friendly, no JSON output blobs, reproducible by construction.
- When to choose which: Streamlit for the fastest stakeholder-facing app from a script; Marimo when the analysis itself must stay reproducible AND interactive (exploration that becomes the deliverable).

```python
# marimo notebook cells (each @app.cell is one reactive unit)
import marimo as mo
app = mo.App()

@app.cell
def _():
    import pandas as pd
    df = pd.read_csv('orders_clean.parquet')
    return (df,)

@app.cell
def _(df):
    region = mo.ui.dropdown(df['region'].unique().tolist(), label='Region')
    return (region,)

@app.cell
def _(df, region):
    view = df[df['region'] == region.value]
    mo.md(f"### {region.value}: {len(view)} orders, ${view['amount'].sum():,.0f} revenue")
    return
